{
  "answer": "The moral error theorist would say that moral claims about stealing, such as \"stealing is wrong,\" are intended to be true but can never achieve truth because no moral properties exist as truth-makers for those claims. Therefore, they cannot endorse the claim that stealing is morally wrong at least sometimes. However, this does not mean they approve of stealing; they may oppose or support stealing for non-moral reasons, such as legal, social, personal, or property-rights considerations.",
  "source_citations": [
    {
      "source_id": "obp0125-11-section7-native-q10-development",
      "quote": "The moral error theorist believes that our moral claims regarding the ethics of stealing are intended to be true, but can never achieve truth because no moral properties exist as truth-makers for those moral claims."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "obp0125-11-section7-native-q10-development",
      "quote": "Importantly, just because the moral error theorist cannot endorse the claim that “stealing can be morally wrong at least sometimes”, this does not entail a love of stealing on their part."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "obp0125-11-section7-native-q10-development",
      "quote": "The moral error theorist may have a non-moral reason for opposing stealing on many occasions, or indeed supporting stealing on other occasions."
    }
  ],
  "uncertainty": "None beyond what is explicitly stated in the provided source.",
  "assumptions": "The question refers to the moral error theorist as described in the provided source; 'error-theorist' is interpreted as the moral error theorist."
}

